# Nuclear 32 — The tritium β spectrum, its mean energy and the ft value
#
# (Krane, Introductory Nuclear Physics, §9.2–9.3, eqs. 9.25–9.33 and Fig. 9.3;
#  Wong 2nd ed. §5.4; Konopinski, The Theory of Beta Radioactivity ch. 1.)
# ³H → ³He + e⁻ + ν̄ has Q = 18.591 keV and t½ = 12.32 yr. For an allowed transition
# the electron spectrum in kinetic energy T is
#     N(T) ∝ F(Z, T) p E (Q − T)²,     E = T + m c²,  p c = √(E² − m²c⁴),
# with the non-relativistic Fermi function for the daughter charge Z = 2,
#     F = 2πη / (1 − e^(−2πη)),   η = Z α E / (p c)  (= Z α / β).
#   (a) The mean kinetic energy ⟨T⟩ = ∫ T N dT / ∫ N dT (measured about 5.7 keV),
#       and the same without the Coulomb correction (F = 1).
#   (b) The most probable kinetic energy (the peak of N(T)): solve dN/dT = 0.
#   (c) The dimensionless Fermi integral f = ∫₁^{W₀} F p W (W₀ − W)² dW (W = E/mc²,
#       p in units of m c), and log₁₀(f t½) with t½ in seconds (tabulated: 3.05, a
#       superallowed mirror transition).

Q = 18.591 keV
mc2 = m_e c²
Z = 2
# F must be one line (with `where`) so that d/dT N works below: written over several
# lines, `d/dT N` fails with "can't differentiate through F", an error with no line number
F(T) = 2π η / (1 - exp(-2π η)) where η = Z α (T + mc2) / √((T + mc2)² - mc2²)
N(T) = F(T) √((T + mc2)² - mc2²) (T + mc2) (Q - T)²
N0(T) = √((T + mc2)² - mc2²) (T + mc2) (Q - T)²

norm = ∫ N(T) dT from 0 keV to Q
T_mean = (∫ T N(T) dT from 0 keV to Q) / norm
T_mean0 = (∫ T N0(T) dT from 0 keV to Q) / (∫ N0(T) dT from 0 keV to Q)
print "(a) <T> =", T_mean in keV to 8 digits, " without Coulomb =", T_mean0 in keV to 8 digits

dN = d/dT N
solve dN(Tp) = 0 keV^3 for Tp from 0.5 keV to 15 keV
print "(b) most probable T =", Tp in keV to 8 digits

W0 = 1 + Q / mc2
FW(W) = 2π Z α W / √(W² - 1) / (1 - exp(-2π Z α W / √(W² - 1)))
f = ∫ FW(W) √(W² - 1) W (W0 - W)² dW from 1 to W0
t_half = 12.32 yr
print "(c) W0 =", W0 to 10 digits, " f =", f to 8 digits, " log10(f t) =", log10(f t_half / (1 s)) to 5 digits
